    "content": "The CBOE Volatility Index (VIX) is a real-time market index representing the market's expectation of 30-day forward-looking volatility of the S&P 500® Index (SPX), often referred to as Wall Street's \"fear gauge\". As of mid-April 2026, the VIX experienced a significant collapse, dropping to around 16–18 levels from higher, indicating decreased market panic. It is calculated using SPX option prices and generally has an inverse relationship with the S&P 500.Key Aspects of the VIX:Purpose: Measures market risk and investor sentiment, with higher values indicating greater fear and volatility (often >30) and lower values (often <20) indicating stability.Calculation: Uses a 30-day, constant, weighted measure of SPX options with Friday expirations.Behavior: The VIX is mean-reverting, meaning it tends to return to its historical average rather than staying high or low permanently.Trading: While you cannot buy the VIX directly, investors trade it through futures, options, and exchange-traded products (ETPs) to hedge against market declines.Recent Performance (April 2026):Following a 44% crash in the three weeks ending April 17, 2026, the VIX hit a nine-week low of 16.87, signalling a shift toward lower volatility. History suggests this type of rapid drop in the VIX can be positive for equity market performance.",
